EDP/EaaS, there are several ways to onboard a device. I would separate them into how the device gets its identity/credentials and how the device connects to your platform.

1. Manufacturing / Pre-Provisioned Onboarding

Device is provisioned before it reaches the customer.

Manufacturing
    ↓
Generate Device Identity
    ↓
Install Certificate + Private Key
    ↓
Ship Device
    ↓
Device boots
    ↓
Connects to EDP
    ↓
ONLINE

Example:

device-001
certificate: device-001.crt
private-key: device-001.key

Good for: production IoT devices, large fleets.

Pros

Strong security
Fully automatic
No manual onboarding

Cons

Requires manufacturing/provisioning infrastructure
Certificate/key handling becomes important
2. Claim Code / Registration Code

This is probably the best approach for your EDP MVP.

Device starts with a temporary identity:

Edge Device
    ↓
Generate claim code
    ↓
"EDP-8F4K92"

User enters the code in your EDP console:

EDP Console
    ↓
Add Device
    ↓
Enter Claim Code
    ↓
Approve

Then the device receives its permanent identity/credentials.

Temporary Identity
       ↓
Claim
       ↓
Permanent Device Identity
       ↓
Secure Connection

This is similar to how many device-management systems solve the "how do I associate this physical device with my account?" problem.

3. QR Code Onboarding

Very useful for physical devices.

Device
 ┌──────────────┐
 │              │
 │   QR CODE    │
 │              │
 └──────────────┘

The QR code could contain:

device_id
claim_token
manufacturing_info

User scans:

Phone/Web
   ↓
Scan QR
   ↓
EDP
   ↓
Approve Device

Good for: Raspberry Pi, gateways, industrial devices, field installations.

4. Manual Device Registration

Admin creates the device manually.

EDP Console

Device ID:     NUC-001
Device Type:   Edge Gateway
Hardware:      Intel NUC
Location:      Tractor-001

Then the device gets credentials.

Good for: development/testing.

Not ideal for: thousands of devices.

5. Bootstrap Certificate → Permanent Certificate

This is a more production-grade approach.

Device
  │
  │ Bootstrap Certificate
  ▼
EDP
  │
  │ Verify
  ▼
Device Identity Service
  │
  │ Issue permanent certificate
  ▼
Device
  │
  │ Permanent certificate
  ▼
EDP

This gives you a strong zero-touch provisioning architecture.

For your EDP, I'd build these in this order
MVP — Start here

Claim Code onboarding

              EDP
               │
         Device Registry
               │
               │
          Claim Code
               │
               ▼
          Edge Agent
               │
          Linux Device

Your demo becomes:

Install Edge Agent → generate claim code → enter code in EDP → device securely registers → device appears ONLINE.

Phase 2

Add:

QR Code

QR → Claim → Provision → ONLINE
Phase 3

Add:

Certificate-based zero-touch provisioning

Factory → Device Identity → EDP → Automatic Onboarding
Phase 4

Add support for different device classes:

                  EDP
                   │
       ┌───────────┼───────────┐
       │           │           │
     ESP32        Linux       Jetson
     MCU          Edge        ARM64
       │           │           │
   Firmware      Agent       Agent
One important architectural decision

Don't make onboarding = MQTT connection.

Instead, model onboarding as a lifecycle:

UNKNOWN
   ↓
CLAIMED
   ↓
PROVISIONING
   ↓
PROVISIONED
   ↓
CONNECTED
   ↓
ONLINE

That gives you a clean foundation for everything you'll add later:

Onboarding
    ↓
Identity
    ↓
OTA
    ↓
Monitoring
    ↓
Configuration
    ↓
Remote Operations




Not exactly. They solve a similar onboarding problem, but they are different mechanisms.

The easiest distinction is:

Claim code = proves that a user is allowed to claim the device.
Bootstrap certificate = proves that the device itself is allowed to join the platform.

Compare them
	Claim Code	Bootstrap Certificate
Main purpose	Device ownership/claiming	Device authentication
Usually entered by	User/admin	Device automatically
Secret type	One-time token/code	Cryptographic certificate + private key
Device initially has	Temporary identity/token	Bootstrap certificate + private key
Human interaction	Usually yes	Usually no
Security	Good if implemented correctly	Stronger
Automation	Semi-automatic	Zero-touch possible
Best for	MVP / field onboarding	Production manufacturing
Claim Code

Imagine your Jetson arrives at a customer site.

Jetson
   │
   │ generates/has claim code
   ▼
EDP: "Add Device"
   │
   │ User enters:
   │ CLAIM-7F92K
   ▼
EDP verifies claim
   │
   ▼
Device gets permanent credentials
   │
   ▼
ONLINE

The claim code connects the physical device to the customer's EDP account.

Bootstrap Certificate

Here the device already has a cryptographic identity.

Jetson
 │
 │ Bootstrap certificate
 │ + private key
 ▼
EDP
 │
 │ Authenticate
 ▼
Provisioning Service
 │
 │ Issue permanent certificate
 ▼
Jetson
 │
 │ Permanent certificate
 ▼
EDP

There may be zero human interaction.

This is much more suitable when you manufacture/provision thousands of devices.